const topicData = [
  ["Stack","LIFO · Last in, first out","A stack is like a pile of plates. You add a new item at the top and remove only the top item. This rule is called LIFO: Last In, First Out.","Use Push to add a value, Pop to remove the top value, and Peek to inspect it without removing it.","Stack"],
  ["Queue","FIFO · First in, first out","A queue is like people waiting in a line. New items join at the rear, and the oldest item leaves from the front. This is FIFO: First In, First Out.","Enqueue adds at the rear. Dequeue removes from the front.","Queue"],
  ["Linked List","Nodes connected by links","A linked list is a chain of nodes. Each node stores data and a link to the next node. Nodes do not need to sit next to each other in memory.","Insert a value at the head or delete the first node. Follow the arrows to understand the links.","Linked List"],
  ["Binary Tree","At most two children","A binary tree is a hierarchy of nodes. Every node can have zero, one, or two children, usually called left and right.","Insert values to build a simple level-order binary tree. Notice the parent-child relationships.","Binary Tree"],
  ["Binary Search Tree","Smaller left · larger right","A Binary Search Tree (BST) is a binary tree with an ordering rule: values smaller than a node go left; larger values go right. This makes searching efficient when the tree is balanced.","Insert values one at a time and follow each comparison. Search by moving left or right.","Binary Search Tree"],
  ["AVL Tree","A self-balancing BST","An AVL tree is a Binary Search Tree that keeps its height balanced. For every node, the height difference between its left and right subtrees must be -1, 0, or 1.","Insert values and observe when a rotation is needed to restore balance. This teaching demo shows the balance idea and rotation type.","AVL Tree"],
  ["Expression Tree","Operators and operands as nodes","An expression tree represents a mathematical expression as a tree. Operators such as + and * are parent nodes; numbers or variables are leaf nodes.","Enter a simple expression such as A+B*C. The tree groups the higher-precedence multiplication first.","Expression Tree"],
  ["Infix to Postfix","Convert operator notation","Infix puts the operator between values, such as A+B. Postfix puts the operator after its values, such as AB+. A stack temporarily holds operators so precedence and parentheses are respected.","Enter an expression such as A+B*C or (A+B)*C, then convert it. * has higher precedence than +.","Infix to Postfix"],
  ["Postfix Expression Evaluation","Evaluate using a stack","In postfix notation, the operator comes after its operands. Read left to right: push numbers; when an operator appears, pop the right operand and then the left operand, calculate, and push the answer.","Enter a space-separated expression such as 5 2 3 * +. Each stack change is shown.","Postfix Expression Evaluation"],
  ["Tower of Hanoi","Move disks between rods","Tower of Hanoi has three rods and disks of different sizes. Move the whole tower from the first rod to the last. Move one disk at a time, and never place a larger disk on a smaller one.","Choose a small number of disks and execute. The simulator displays the legal moves in order.","Tower of Hanoi"],
  ["Graph Traversals","BFS and DFS","A graph contains vertices (points) connected by edges (links). Traversal means visiting reachable vertices systematically. BFS explores nearby vertices first; DFS follows one path deeply before backtracking.","Run BFS or DFS from vertex A. Watch the visited order and the queue or stack frontier.","Graph Traversals"],
  ["B-tree","Balanced multiway search tree","A B-tree is a balanced search tree in which a node can hold several sorted keys and have several children. It is useful for databases and storage because it reduces disk reads.","Insert keys into a simplified B-tree illustration. Keys inside each node remain sorted; nodes split when full.","B-tree"],
  ["Hashing – Separate Chaining","A list at each table index","Hashing maps a key to a table index using a hash function. Two keys can map to the same index; this is called a collision. Separate chaining stores colliding keys in a list at that index.","Insert keys and watch colliding values share a bucket.","Separate Chaining"],
  ["Hashing – Open Addressing","Find another empty slot","Open addressing handles a collision by searching for another empty slot in the same table. All keys stay inside the table itself.","Insert keys and watch the probe sequence. Choose a probing strategy in the simulator.","Open Addressing"],
  ["Hashing – Linear Probing","Check the next slot","Linear probing resolves a collision by checking the next slot, then the next, wrapping around at the end of the table.","Insert keys. After a collision, the simulator checks index + 1, index + 2, and so on.","Linear Probing"],
  ["Hashing – Quadratic Probing","Use squared offsets","Quadratic probing checks positions using squared offsets after a collision, commonly h(k)+1², h(k)+2², and so on, modulo table size.","Insert keys and inspect the changing probe positions.","Quadratic Probing"],
  ["Hashing – Double Hashing","Use a second hash function","Double hashing uses a second hash function to decide the step size after a collision. This helps spread keys more evenly than repeatedly checking adjacent slots.","Insert keys and follow the second hash function's step size.","Double Hashing"],
  ["Rehashing","Resize and rebuild a hash table","Rehashing creates a larger table and inserts the existing keys again using the new table size. The index may change because the hash calculation changes.","Insert several keys until the load factor is high, then rehash into a larger table.","Rehashing"],
  ["Linear Search","Check one by one","Linear search starts at the first item and checks each value until the target is found or the list ends. It works even when the data is not sorted.","Enter comma-separated values and a target. Each comparison is highlighted.","Linear Search"],
  ["Binary Search","Halve a sorted search range","Binary search works on sorted data. It checks the middle value, then discards the half that cannot contain the target. It repeats until found or no range remains.","Enter sorted values and a target. Watch low, middle, and high move after every comparison.","Binary Search"],
  ["Internal Sorting","Data fits in main memory","Internal sorting means the complete data set fits in the computer's main memory (RAM), so an algorithm can work directly on it.","Explore an in-memory sorting example and compare the values before and after sorting.","Internal Sorting"],
  ["Bubble Sort","Compare adjacent values","Bubble sort repeatedly compares neighbouring values. If a pair is in the wrong order, it swaps them. After each full pass, the largest remaining value moves to the end.","Enter values and watch every comparison and swap, one step at a time.","Bubble Sort"],
  ["Shell Sort","Sort with decreasing gaps","Shell sort improves on insertion sort by first comparing values far apart. It gradually reduces the gap until the final pass has gap 1.","Watch the array being compared using decreasing gaps.","Shell Sort"],
  ["External Sorting","Data is bigger than RAM","External sorting is used when the data is too large to fit in main memory. The data is split into smaller chunks, sorted separately, saved, and merged.","See how sorted runs are created and combined.","External Sorting"],
  ["Multiway Merge","Merge several sorted runs","A multiway merge combines more than two sorted runs at once. It repeatedly chooses the smallest front value among the runs.","Enter sorted runs separated by semicolons, for example 1,4;2,5;3,6.","Multiway Merge"],
  ["Polyphase Merge","Merge runs across files","Polyphase merge is an external sorting method. It distributes sorted runs across files in a planned way and repeatedly merges runs while rotating the output file.","Explore a simplified three-file merge sequence and see how runs move between files.","Polyphase Merge"]
];
